From df473ea6d6056cf607920ee2224f6ca55fbbb152 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: xiao bo <peterwillcn@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] ath79: wdt: clamp the timeout to what the counter can express

The watchdog counter runs off the AHB clock, so the longest timeout it can
express is 0xffffffff / wdt_freq, which is 25 seconds on an AR7161.  procd
always asks for 30 seconds and then logs:

  WDT failed to set timeout 30s: Invalid argument

and keeps the previous value, which looks like a failure during sysupgrade
although the watchdog itself works.

Clamp larger requests to the maximum instead of rejecting them.  The value
actually programmed is reported back through WDIOC_SETTIMEOUT, so callers see
the real timeout.

--- a/drivers/watchdog/ath79_wdt.c
+++ b/drivers/watchdog/ath79_wdt.c
@@ -111,9 +111,21 @@ static inline void ath79_wdt_disable(voi
 
 static int ath79_wdt_set_timeout(int val)
 {
-	if (val < 1 || val > max_timeout)
+	/*
+	 * The counter runs off the AHB clock, so the longest timeout it can
+	 * express is 0xffffffff / wdt_freq - 25 seconds on an AR7161.  Clamp
+	 * larger requests instead of rejecting them: callers such as procd
+	 * always ask for 30 seconds and then only log an error and keep the
+	 * previous value, which looks like a failure although the watchdog
+	 * works fine.  The value actually used is reported back to the caller
+	 * by WDIOC_SETTIMEOUT.
+	 */
+	if (val < 1)
 		return -EINVAL;
 
+	if (val > max_timeout)
+		val = max_timeout;
+
 	timeout = val;
 	ath79_wdt_keepalive();
 
